Today I resumed working on the URL handler. Initially, I was splitting the URL into different components directly inside the constructor, but I decided that this was not a good design. Instead, I created a separate function named split_url() that parses the URL into its different components, and I simply call this function from the constructor. This keeps the constructor cleaner and makes the parsing logic reusable.

After that, I implemented the resolve_url() function. This function takes two arguments: the base URL and the extracted URL that needs to be resolved. I handled four different cases. If the extracted URL is already an absolute URL, it is returned as it is. If it is protocol-relative, I add the protocol from the base URL. If it is root-relative, I append it to the website's root. Finally, if it is a normal relative URL such as "contact" or "../contact", I resolve it relative to the base URL and return the resulting absolute URL.

Once the resolver was completed, I integrated it into the crawl() function. While doing this, I noticed that it would be useful to create a URL object immediately after popping a URL from the frontier. Since this URL becomes the base URL for all links extracted from that page, having its components already parsed makes the comparisons and resolution logic much simpler. After making these changes, I tested the crawler, and the URL resolution worked correctly.

Next, I started implementing the URL normalization function. Inside this function, I created a temporary URL object using the URL returned by the resolver. I then performed several normalization steps, including ensuring that an empty path is replaced with /, converting the scheme and host to lowercase, removing fragments, resolving .. path components, and removing default port numbers.

After integrating the normalizer into the crawler, I encountered a runtime error. The error message pointed to operator[](size_type) inside basic_string.h and reported that _pos <= size() had failed. From this, I understood that somewhere in my code I was trying to access a character outside the bounds of a std::string.

My first suspicion was the link_extractor() function because I had recently changed one of its loop conditions to iterate until html.size() - 7. However, after checking the logic carefully, I realized that this was not the actual cause of the problem. I then suspected the split_url() function because it accesses characters using url[i], but after further testing, I found that the same error still occurred elsewhere.

To identify the real problem, I decided to debug the program using gdb. The debugger showed me the exact line where the program crashed. I discovered that the HTML string was sometimes empty, so html.size() was equal to zero. Since html.size() returns an unsigned size_t, calculating html.size() - 6 caused an unsigned underflow, producing a very large positive value instead of a negative number. As a result, the loop condition became true, and the program attempted to access characters beyond the end of the string.

I fixed this bug by changing the loop condition to i + 6 < html.size(), which correctly checks that there are enough remaining characters before accessing them. After making this change, the runtime error was resolved, and the crawler continued working as expected